{"author":{"id":"a3a282966ced55effb4560271fcd98e13241af24ea76b00ec76a0f18154bc8ee"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790104456,"nonce":"73NVXdH0G1T0muKGmESJdy8TbyE=","message":"Revised 2026-09-22 after review (Luis: why aren't relations SetField, and where are Add/RemoveMultiField?). AddRelation/RemoveRelation are gone; a relation is a field whose kind says the value is an issue id (kind relation = SetField of one id; kind multi-relation = a list of ids). In their place, AddValue{key,item} (code 10) and RemoveValue{key,item} (code 11) give set semantics on ANY list-valued field (labels, components, reviewers, watchers, blocks) so two concurrent adds both win, which a list-valued SetField (last writer wins) cannot do and which git-bug's LabelChangeOp did. Items are canonical (compacted) JSON, the list is kept sorted by bytes, adds are idempotent, remove leaves [] rather than unsetting, null is not an item, title is not a list. SetField on a list field means replace-all; a concurrent SetField and AddValue resolve in lamport order (replace vs add in JSON Patch terms). The Snapshot lost its Relations slice; cache and cmdjson followed; the CLI is: git work issue set ID KEY --add ITEM --remove ITEM (an unambiguous issue prefix resolves to the full id). Op set is now Create(1) AddComment(3) EditComment(6) NoOp(7) SetMetadata(8) SetField(9) AddValue(10) RemoveValue(11). Design: configurable-schema.md D2 and D4.","files":null}]}